outputs.exact_statement:
There are polynomial coordinates $p',s',F',J$ on $A$ such that $$\label{con:candidate}
 x=(s')^2-J^2+p'F',\qquad
 p=p'-2x\bigl(x(s'+J)+p'J\bigr).$$ Thus, under the identification $(p',s',F',J)=(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4)$, the element $p$ is the polynomial $f$ in Theorem <a href="#thm:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:main">1</a>.

hypotheses.statement:
In $R=\mathbb C[h,u,v,w]$, put $$\label{eq:xyz}
  \begin{aligned}
    x&=u^3+hv,\qquad y=-u^2+hw,\\
    s&=S(h,u,v,w)
       :=2u^3v+3u^4w+h(v^2-3u^2w^2)+h^2w^3.
  \end{aligned}$$
